[agent] I've completed the task. Here's what I did and found:
[agent] ## Approach
[agent] Used the Hopsworks platform's **descriptive statistics** capability (the cluster's `create_feature_monitoring` service is disabled — `errorCode 270234`). I ingested the daily stream into feature groups with **one commit per day**, so Hopsworks computed per-day batch statistics (each commit's stats had `count=150`, i.e. one day's rows) via its server-side statistics job. I then read back the per-day mean/stddev for every feature via `fg.get_statistics()` and tracked each feature's daily distribution across all 90 days.
[agent] ## Finding
[agent] **Feature `f3` drifted**, with onset **2026-03-11**:
[agent] | Window | f3 mean | f3 stddev |
[agent] |---|---|---|
[agent] | Jan 01 → Mar 10 | ~4.77 (flat) | ~0.67 |
[agent] | Mar 11 → Mar 31 | ~7.45 (flat) | ~0.68 |
[agent] The transition is sharp: Mar 09 = 4.757, Mar 10 = 4.777 (old level), then Mar 11 = 7.433 and it stays at the new level for the rest of the stream — a clean mean shift of ~+2.7 with unchanged variance. Every other feature (f1, f2, f4, f5, f6) stayed flat in both mean and stddev across all 90 days.
[agent] ## Deliverable
[agent] `submission/answers.json`:
[agent] ```json
[agent] {"feature": "f3", "onset": "2026-03-11"}
[agent] ```
[agent] **Note on the interface:** Per-day windowed statistics were not directly obtainable from the Python SDK — feature monitoring (the natural tool, with detection/reference windows) is disabled cluster-wide, `fs.sql` (Hive) is unsupported on client ≥4.0, and Arrow Flight only accepts server-signed query payloads (no ad-hoc aggregation SQL). I worked within these limits by using per-day commits to get native per-batch statistics. The main cost was that Hudi/Delta upsert insert latency grows with table size (~8s → ~40s per commit), so I split the stream across three feature groups to keep per-commit statistics fast.
